<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/foundational/weathering-erosion-and-landforms">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    The surface of the Earth is <strong>a demolition and redistribution project running for
    4.5 billion years</strong>. Uplift builds mountains; three processes tear them down and
    file the pieces. <strong>Weathering</strong> breaks rock apart <em>in place</em> —
    with ice, water chemistry, temperature swings and roots. <strong>Erosion</strong> picks
    the pieces up and moves them — by rivers, glaciers, wind and gravity. <strong>Deposition</strong>
    sets them down again — in deltas, dune fields, sea floors — sorted by size, layer by
    layer. Read any landscape — a valley's shape, a cliff's strata, a beach's grain — and
    you are reading which process dominated, for how long. Geomorphology, the study of
    landforms, is essentially grammar for that reading.
  </p>
  <p>
    The scale is worth pausing on. The Grand Canyon is a kilometre of rock removed by one
    river and its tributaries; the Himalaya's erosion feeds the Bengal Fan, a cone of
    sediment 16 km thick on the Indian Ocean floor — the largest body of sediment on the
    planet. Nothing in human construction compares; yet the same processes, sped up, are
    what erosion-control engineers and farmers fight every season.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="break, move, sort, read" widget="equationStep" config={weatherCfg} />

  <h2>Mechanical and chemical weathering</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Mechanical (physical) weathering</strong> breaks rock without changing its
    chemistry. The champion is <strong>frost wedging</strong>: water seeps into cracks and
    expands ~9% when it freezes, prying with forces up to ~200 MPa — enough to split
    granite. Repeat nightly through a mountain winter and cliff faces shed scree. Its
    cousins: <strong>thermal cycling</strong> (daily heating and cooling stressing minerals
    with different expansion rates, the desert's shatter), <strong>exfoliation</strong>
    (pressure release as overburden erodes away lets granite domes — Half Dome, Sugarloaf —
    peel in onion sheets), <strong>abrasion</strong> (grains sandblasting each other), and
    <strong>root wedging</strong> (a tree root widening a crack by slow hydraulic force).
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Chemical weathering</strong> attacks the minerals themselves, and rainwater —
    weak carbonic acid from dissolved CO₂ — is the reagent. <strong>Dissolution</strong>
    simply dissolves soluble minerals: halite and gypsum go fast; <strong>calcite</strong>
    goes slowly but inexorably, which is why limestone terrains develop
    <strong>karst</strong> — caves, sinkholes, disappearing streams, limestone pavements —
    and why marble statues weather faster than granite ones.
    <strong>Hydrolysis</strong> attacks silicates: feldspar + acidic water → clay minerals
    + dissolved ions — the reaction that turns nearly every igneous rock into clay and
    makes clay the single most abundant weathering product. <strong>Oxidation</strong>
    rusts iron-bearing minerals (the reds and browns of weathered rock and rusty soil).
    The two kinds of weathering <em>feed each other</em>: cracks from frost give water
    new surfaces to attack; chemical rotting crumbles rock for ice to split — a feedback
    that demolishes whole mountainsides, and the reason fresh granite corestones sit
    inside rotten-granite "saprolite" on tropical hills.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "erosion and weathering are the same thing." They are
    separate stages: weathering is <em>in place</em> (the rock is weakened and fragmented
    where it sits); erosion is <em>transport</em> (the pieces are carried away). A crumbled
    but unmoved talus slope shows weathering without erosion; a polished, clean-cut
    glacial valley shows erosion that outran weathering.
  </p>

  <h2>Erosion and deposition by water, wind and ice</h2>
  <p>
    Each transport agent has a signature. <strong>Water</strong> is the planet's chief
    hauler: a stream erodes by hydraulic action (water hammering into cracks), abrasion
    (its sediment load sandpapering the bed) and solution, carrying material as bedload
    (rolling gravel), suspended load (silt and clay) and dissolved load. Where a stream
    slows — inside meander bends, at a mountain front, at the sea — it <strong>deposits
    by size</strong>: fast flows drop big particles, slow flows drop fine, so downstream
    sorting runs gravel → sand → silt → clay. That sorting builds every alluvial fan,
    floodplain and <strong>delta</strong>, and the same size-law applied by the sea builds
    beaches (wave-sorted sand) and mudflats. <strong>Ice</strong> is the bulldozer:
    glacier ice, under hundreds of metres of its own weight, drags frozen debris across
    bedrock, <em>plucking</em> blocks loose and <em>polishing</em> what remains with
    rock-flour. Glaciers carve the unmistakable signature: <strong>U-shaped valleys</strong>
    (versus the stream's V), cirques, arêtes, fjords, and deposits with <em>no</em> sorting
    at all — moraine, till: a chaotic mix from clay to house-sized boulders, the one
    sediment where the size-law conspicuously fails, because ice does not sort.
    <strong>Wind</strong> is the specialist in dry, unvegetated places: it wins only sand
    and dust, building <strong>dunes</strong> whose cross-layers record the prevailing
    wind, and <strong>loess</strong> — vast silt blankets blown out of glacial outwash
    (China's loess plateau is hundreds of metres thick) that become, ironically, superb
    farmland. Coasts add their own clause: waves erode headlands into cliffs, stacks and
    arches, and sort their sediment into the world's beaches.
  </p>

  <h2>Sedimentary rock types</h2>
  <p>
    Deposited sediment does not stay loose: burial compacts it and minerals precipitating
    between grains (usually calcite, silica or iron oxide) cement it into rock —
    <strong>lithification</strong> — and the resulting rocks sort into three clans.
    <strong>Clastic</strong> rocks are made of broken rock particles, classified by grain
    size: <strong>conglomerate</strong> (rounded gravel — a fast, short journey),
    <strong>sandstone</strong> (sand — deserts, beaches, rivers), <strong>shale</strong>
    (mud, compacted into fissile sheets — the calm deep-water record, and the most
    abundant sedimentary rock of all). <strong>Chemical</strong> rocks precipitated from
    solution: <strong>limestone</strong> (calcite from warm shallow seas — much of it
    shell debris), <strong>evaporites</strong> (halite and gypsum from drying basins — the
    Mediterranean nearly dried to one ~6 million years ago, leaving kilometre-thick salt),
    and chert (silica). <strong>Organic</strong> rocks are biology-made: <strong>coal</strong>
    (compacted swamp forest, carbonised), <strong>chalk</strong> (coccolithophore chalk
    skies of the Cretaceous), and reef limestones. Every sedimentary rock, in other words,
    is a compressed photograph of a place and moment — gravel bars, dune fields, seabeds —
    stacked in order.
  </p>

  <h2>Sedimentary structures and environments</h2>
  <p>
    The reading gets precise because sediments preserve <strong>structures</strong> — the
    shapes imposed while they were deposited. <strong>Bedding</strong> (layers) is the
    default; each bed is one episode. <strong>Cross-bedding</strong> — layers inclined
    inside a bed — is the dune's and the river bar's handwriting: the inclination records
    current direction, so ancient sandstones can be read as fossilised wind fields.
    <strong>Ripple marks</strong> preserve wave or current action; <strong>mud cracks</strong>
    prove a surface dried in air (a lake margin, a floodplain) — a palaeoclimate clue in
    one crack; <strong>graded beds</strong> (coarse below, fine above) record underwater
    avalanches (turbidites); <strong>ripple-and-mud-crack pairs</strong> are a tidal
    flat's fingerprint. Fossils are the ultimate sedimentary structure: only sedimentary
    rocks reliably host them, and the fossils plus structures together identify the
    <strong>depositional environment</strong> — the discipline called facies analysis.
    Worked example: a rock unit showing cross-bedded sandstone with wind-rounded grains,
    no fossils but reptile footprints — a fossilised desert dune field, exactly as
    interpreted for the Permian sandstones of the American Southwest. Whole extinct
    geographies are reconstructed from this grammar, which is how we know oceans sat
    where the Alps now stand.
  </p>

  <h2>Mass wasting: gravity's own department</h2>
  <p>
    Between weathering and river transport sits a transport agent the syllabus files
    separately: <strong>mass wasting</strong> — the downslope movement of rock and soil
    under gravity alone. The classes are named by speed and water content, and each has
    a readable scar. <strong>Rockfalls</strong> (instant, from cliffs — the talus cones
    beneath them); <strong>landslides and rockslides</strong> (a coherent mass on a
    failure plane — the 1963 Vajont disaster moved 270 million m³ of mountainside into a
    reservoir in under a minute); <strong>slumps</strong> (rotational, curved scarps —
    the hillside slipping like a stiff cake); <strong>mudflows and debris flows</strong>
    (water-saturated, fast, channel-hugging — volcano-lahars buried Armero, Colombia, in
    1985); and <strong>soil creep</strong> (centimetres a year, visible as tilted fence
    posts and curved tree trunks — slow enough to ignore and steady enough to dominate
    hillside budgets). The trigger is usually water: it adds weight, lubricates failure
    planes, and dissolves the cement between grains — which is why the hazard maps that
    route roads and housing are, in essence, hydrology maps. Deforestation and slope
    cutting move the odds; the 2006 Philippines Guinsaugon slide followed ten days of
    rain on a logged slope.
  </p>
  <p>
    Soil deserves its own paragraph because it is weathering's end product and
    agriculture's substrate. A mature <strong>soil profile</strong> layers the results:
    an O horizon of litter; an A horizon (topsoil) of mineral mixed with humus — the
    biologically active layer; a B horizon where clay and iron leached from above
    accumulate; a C horizon of rotten parent rock; then bedrock. The profile takes
    centuries to millimetres-per-year to build and one bare winter to lose, which is why
    contour ploughing, terracing and cover crops are erosion policy as much as agronomy.
    Soil type records the climate that made it — deep red laterites in the wet tropics
    (everything leached but iron), chernozem's metre of black topsoil under grasslands
    (the world's breadbaskets), thin aridosols under deserts — a map of past weather
    drawn in the ground.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Quantitatively, landscape evolution is a <strong>budget</strong>: uplift in, denudation
    out. Typical erosion rates on stable cratons run ~10–100 metres per million years;
    active mountain ranges shed 0.1–1 mm per year — enough to remove an Alps in ~50
    million years if uplift stopped, which is why mountain ranges are not eras but
    standoffs. Rivers do most of the moving (~19 billion tonnes of sediment to the oceans
    annually), glaciers punch far above their coverage, and the <strong>human
    acceleration</strong> is now the headline: agriculture and construction move more
    sediment than all natural processes combined (Wilkinson's 2005 estimate — the
    "Anthropocene" argument in one measurement), while eroding farmland at 10–100× the
    rate soil forms. Soil itself is weathering plus biology — centuries to build, one bad
    storm to lose.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two deeper nuances. First, <strong>climate writes the style</strong>: the same
    granite weathers into rounded boulders in hot wet tropics (chemical dominance),
    angular debris in polar ranges (frost dominance), and desert pavement under
    wind-only regimes — geomorphology is palaeoclimatology written on the ground, which
    is how the ancient glacial striations of India, Africa and Brazil helped prove
    continental drift. Second, the <strong>sedimentary archive is selective</strong>:
    erosion erases as much as deposition records (the "incompleteness of the fossil and
    stratigraphic record" — most of deep time is missing from any one place), so reading
    the archive requires correlating records across continents — the method the
    geologic-time lesson takes up. The Grand Canyon is the best classroom for both
    lessons at once: a kilometre-deep absence, whose walls preserve 1.5 billion years of
    shallow seas, deserts and deltas in the order they happened.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Press &amp; Siever, Understanding Earth; Wilkinson, Geology (2005) on human sediment movement; Milliman &amp; Farnsworth, Runoff in the Coastal Zone; OpenStax, An Introduction to Geology (ch. 5).</p>
</TopicPage>
